## 4. The Jute Rope and Post Trellis (Cheapest and Easiest)

This is the most beginner-friendly trellis of all — just wooden posts, jute twine, and looped ties. It looks rustic and charming, costs almost nothing, and takes under an hour to set up.

[![Simple jute rope trellis with looped twine ties supporting young raspberry canes in a raised garden bed]({{ site.baseurl }}/assets/images/home-decor/jute-rope-post-trellis-raspberries.jpg)](https://pin.it/xmgq2860Y)

### Materials (Under $20 Total)

- 2 wooden posts (2 inches x 4 ft) — $6 at Home Depot
- Natural jute twine (200 ft roll) — $7 on Amazon (brand: PARACORD PLANET or Gardener's Blue Ribbon)
- Hammer or mallet to drive posts

### How to Set It Up

1. **Drive two posts into the ground**, 4–6 feet apart, at each end of your planting row.
2. **Tie a horizontal line of jute** between posts at 18 inches off the ground.
3. **Add a second line** at 3 feet.
4. **For each plant, cut a 12-inch loop of jute** and hang it from the top line — this is where each cane slides through for support.
5. **Plant raspberries** between the posts, spacing them about 18–24 inches apart.

The hanging loops give each cane individual support without restricting growth. This system is also easy to take down at the end of the season.

