## FAQ

**Q: What is the easiest berry to grow on a trellis?**
Raspberries are generally the easiest. They grow vigorously, bounce back from mistakes, and produce fruit in their second year.

**Q: Can I grow berries in containers on a trellis?**
Yes! Use a pot that is at least 15 inches deep and attach a small bamboo stake or mini trellis. Compact varieties like 'Raspberry Shortcake' or 'Baby Cakes' blackberry are bred specifically for containers.

**Q: How long does a wooden trellis last outdoors?**
Cedar or pressure-treated pine lasts 10–15 years. Untreated pine lasts 3–5 years. Paint or seal with exterior wood stain for longest life.

**Q: When should I set up my trellis?**
Set it up before you plant — it is much easier than trying to install posts around existing canes.

**Q: Do I need to prune berry plants on a trellis?**
Yes. Remove old canes (floricanes) after they fruit each year. This keeps the plant productive and prevents disease.
